From: rive
Subject: [PATCH 0072] copy a v128 whole where unaligned access is off

Targets without unaligned access, of which we build 32-bit ARM, store a
v128 into the frame one 32-bit lane at a time, and that macro read its
value argument again for every lane. The stack copy hands it a read of the
source slot, so when the destination sat one to three cells above the
source, the later lanes were read back from cells the earlier stores had
just overwritten. The loader emits exactly that copy when a block that
takes parameters ends with a single v128 result lying below the block's
result slot, so such a block returned a vector with its first lanes
repeated. The value is read once now. Every 64-bit target copies the whole
vector in one assignment and never had the fault.

diff --git a/core/iwasm/common/wasm_runtime_common.h b/core/iwasm/common/wasm_runtime_common.h
index 4d0042d..fc76c29 100644
--- a/core/iwasm/common/wasm_runtime_common.h
+++ b/core/iwasm/common/wasm_runtime_common.h
@@ -95,13 +95,16 @@ STORE_V128(void *addr, V128 value)
 
 #else /* WASM_CPU_SUPPORTS_UNALIGNED_ADDR_ACCESS != 0 */
 
+/* rive patch: value is read once, since a stack copy to a slot one to three
+   cells up would otherwise read back lanes it has already written. */
 #define PUT_V128_TO_ADDR(addr, value)        \
     do {                                     \
         uint32 *addr_u32 = (uint32 *)(addr); \
-        addr_u32[0] = (value).i32x4[0];      \
-        addr_u32[1] = (value).i32x4[1];      \
-        addr_u32[2] = (value).i32x4[2];      \
-        addr_u32[3] = (value).i32x4[3];      \
+        V128 value_v128 = (value);           \
+        addr_u32[0] = value_v128.i32x4[0];   \
+        addr_u32[1] = value_v128.i32x4[1];   \
+        addr_u32[2] = value_v128.i32x4[2];   \
+        addr_u32[3] = value_v128.i32x4[3];   \
     } while (0)
 
 #define PUT_I64_TO_ADDR(addr, value)         \
